\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.1}{\ignorespaces Base models and their test cross-entropy on the fixed 1.5M-position test set.* The reference model \texttt {W128 H256} is the checkpoint whose head is replaced by expert heads in the MoE experiments, and whose gradients define the routing signal.}}{48}{table.caption.17}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.2}{\ignorespaces Mini-Batch K-Means clustering diagnostics for the three representations and the piece-count reference, on one million training positions. Silhouette is estimated on a $10\,000$-point subsample; the share columns give the smallest and largest bucket as a fraction of the sample.}}{51}{table.caption.18}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.3}{\ignorespaces DBSCAN results, fitted on a $100\,000$-point subsample with \texttt {min\_samples}$=80$ and $\varepsilon $ selected on a quantile grid. The board and L1 runs are fit in a 48-dimensional PCA of the data, the gradient run in the native 48 dimensions, so the rows are not directly comparable across representations. Bucket sizes are the cluster shares of the full one-million-position assignment; the noise column gives the fraction of the $100\,000$-point fit flagged as noise.}}{52}{table.caption.19}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.4}{\ignorespaces Agreement (adjusted Rand index and normalised mutual information) between partitions at $B=8$, computed on one million positions. Higher values indicate closer agreement; the chance baseline is $0$.}}{53}{table.caption.20}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.5}{\ignorespaces Parameter-free centroid routers (\texttt {argmin} cosine similarity to the $k$-means centroids), scored against the Euclidean Mini-Batch $k$-Means labels on one million positions. Chance is $1/B$; the majority dummy ranges from $0.727$ ($B=2$) to $0.172$ ($B=16$) for the L1 labels.}}{58}{table.caption.25}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.6}{\ignorespaces Learned L1-to-L1 dispatchers on one million positions ($90/10$ split), scored against the L1 Mini-Batch $k$-Means labels. Chance is $1/B$; the majority dummy is $0.727$, $0.542$, $0.248$, and $0.172$ for $B=2,4,8,16$ respectively.}}{59}{table.caption.27}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.7}{\ignorespaces MLP dispatchers (hidden width $h$) trained to predict the gradient $k$-means buckets from L1 activations, on one million positions ($90/10$ split). Chance is $1/B$; the majority dummy is $0.560$, $0.361$, $0.184$, and $0.134$ for $B=2,4,8,16$ respectively.}}{60}{table.caption.29}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.8}{\ignorespaces Alignment (ARI/NMI) between the fixed eight-bucket piece-count router and the L1 and gradient $k$-means partitions at each $B$, computed on one million positions.}}{61}{table.caption.30}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.9}{\ignorespaces Dense baselines (linear, single-hidden FFNN, dual-hidden FFNN) with full regression metrics and inference throughput on the 1.5M-position test set. The reference NNUE W128 H256 (174,723 parameters) attains CE 0.6303, beating the dual-hidden FFNN2 H256 at 2.9$\times $ the parameters.}}{64}{table.caption.32}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.10}{\ignorespaces Dispatcher-routed hard MoE (piece-count, L1-clustered, gradient-clustered) on the 50,000-position test subsample. The reference base attains CE 0.6303 and MAE 0.1326 on this subsample. "Dispatcher Top-1" is the MLP dispatcher's accuracy against its own k-means pseudo-labels (L1 or gradient).}}{68}{table.caption.36}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.11}{\ignorespaces End-to-end soft-gated MoE test CE (top-1 and top-2 gating), on the 50,000-position subsample. Base CE 0.6303. Gate entropy $\approx $ ln K and load variance $\approx $ 0 at every setting.}}{68}{table.caption.37}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.12}{\ignorespaces Switch (end-to-end sparse top-1) test CE vs. load-balancing weight $\alpha $, on the 50,000-position subsample. Base CE 0.6303.}}{69}{table.caption.38}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.13}{\ignorespaces Oracle upper bound on the gradient-clustered experts, on the 50,000-position subsample. "Dispatcher CE" is the realised MLP-routed MoE; "Nearest-centroid CE" is the cosine/nearest-centroid routing rule of Section 5.3.3; "Oracle CE" is the true $\arg \min _k \mathcal {L}_k$ routing. The oracle uses the target at inference and is an unachievable upper bound.}}{69}{table.caption.39}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.14}{\ignorespaces Random dispatcher control (uniform random routing over the same experts), on the 50,000-position subsample. Base CE 0.6303; the trained-dispatcher MoE CE is given in Table 5.10.}}{70}{table.caption.40}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.15}{\ignorespaces Best L1-clustered hard-MoE runs on the weaker \texttt {W64 H128} base (CE 0.6537). Each run uses a linear dispatcher and a longer expert fine-tuning budget than Section 5.5.3.}}{70}{table.caption.41}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {5.16}{\ignorespaces KITSUNE on the \texttt {W64 H128} base (CE 0.6537). $B'/K$ is the number of L1 micro-buckets per macro-expert. Best expert CE is the cross-entropy of the strongest expert on the positions assigned to it. The gain column is $(0.6537 - \text {best expert CE}) / 0.6537$. It uses the full-base CE as the reference, so it is not a same-bucket comparison against the base head. The routed mixture CE is the separate column.}}{72}{table.caption.42}%
